<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  @DOCK_MODEL@: dock @DOCK_LETTER@, carrying tag36h11 id @DOCK_TAG_ID@.

  GENERATED FILE - do not edit. The source is dock_template/model.sdf.in, and
  CMakeLists.txt configures it once per dock so that all eight docks share one
  set of numbers. The eight differ only in the two identity textures and the
  name.

  This is the delivery target the rover drives into.

  All numbers there are millimetres; SDF is metres, so every dimension below is
  that value divided by 1000.

  Shape is a shallow trapezoidal bay - a flat back wall carrying the AprilTag
  plus two wings flared out at exactly 45 deg. A flat wall would leave position
  ALONG the wall unobservable to a 2D LiDAR, so a scan match against it slides
  sideways; three segments meeting at two known angles constrain x, y and yaw.
  The 45 deg flare is what makes the dock distinguishable from a straight wall
  (0 deg) and a box corner (90 deg), so do not round it off. The wings are a
  perception feature and a soft limit, NOT a funnel: a skid-steer drivetrain has
  very high lateral friction, so a robot that touches a wing pushes and stalls
  rather than sliding into alignment.

  Frame: origin at floor level on the centre of the back wall's inner face.
  +X out of the dock into the arena (the approach direction), +Y left as seen
  from the dock looking outward, +Z up. The bay occupies positive X, so a robot
  approaching the dock travels in -X.

  All panels are 8 mm thick boxes rather than meshes: no external asset to keep
  in sync, and the small mitre overlap where a wing meets the back wall is
  harmless. Every panel is solid from the floor up - a panel on legs would let
  the LiDAR scan plane pass under it and see two thin posts instead of a wall.

  Three structural panels - middle (back wall), left wing, right wing - all
  300 mm tall. The human-readable letter for dock @DOCK_LETTER@ is a decal on
  the back wall's own free zone (z 200..300) rather than a fourth panel - see
  "letter" below. The AprilTag is the machine-readable identity marker; the
  two cannot drift apart because CMakeLists.txt derives both textures from
  the same DOCK_ASSIGNMENTS entry.

  ==========================================================================
  WHAT THE APPEARANCE IS ALLOWED TO TOUCH

  Everything the robot senses lives in a narrow slice of this model, so the
  styling below is confined to the parts that are provably outside it:

    inner faces, z  80..180  FROZEN. Planar, continuous, light, matte. This is
                             the mandated legal scan-height band and the whole
                             of the LiDAR contract.
    tag plate + margin       FROZEN. y +-62.5, z 67.5..192.5, and nothing
                             decorative below z=200, which leaves 7.5 mm of
                             clearance between the plate's top edge and the
                             accent band's bottom edge.
    inward protrusions
    below z=180              FORBIDDEN. Occlusion or shadow on the tag.
    inner faces, z 200..300  Free - texture and geometry. No legal scan height
                             reaches it. Panels top out at z=300, so this is
                             also the top of the model.
    outer faces              Free. The robot never sees these from the bay.

  So: the accent band (z 200..210) and the letter decal (z 219..291) both
  sit at z >= 200. The panels are 0.90 diffuse, which gives the LiDAR full
  range off the inner faces, and the accent band's pattern gives the OAK-D's
  stereo pair something to correlate on, where a blank wall is close to its
  worst case.

  The one rule that holds everywhere: keep it matte. Gloss anywhere near the tag
  specular-blows-out under arena lights.
-->
<sdf version="1.10">
  <model name="@DOCK_MODEL@">
    <static>true</static>

    <link name="dock_body">

      <!-- ============================ walls ============================ -->

      <!-- Back wall: 8 x 400 x 300, inner face at x=0, spans y -200..+200.
           Deliberately left as one monolithic panel. -->
      <visual name="back_wall_visual">
        <pose>-0.004 0 0.150 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.008 0.400 0.300</size></box></geometry>
        <!-- Off-white and matte: ~90 % reflectivity gives the LiDAR full range,
             where a dark or glossy dock returns weakly. -->
        <material>
          <ambient>0.62 0.62 0.62 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.90 0.90 0.89 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.04 0.04 0.04 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="back_wall_collision">
        <pose>-0.004 0 0.150 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.008 0.400 0.300</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Wings: three panels total for the whole dock (middle, left,
           right), all 300 mm tall, matching the back wall, each wing
           200 mm long.

           Full wing inner face runs (0, 200) -> (141.421, 341.421); local +X
           points at the mouth. The panel centre is the inner-face midpoint
           offset 4 mm along the panel's outward normal, (-sin(yaw), cos(yaw))
           - i.e. (-0.70711, 0.70711) for the left wing's +45 deg yaw - same
           convention as the back wall's -0.004 x offset. Getting the sign of
           that normal wrong (offsetting along the length direction instead,
           (cos(yaw), sin(yaw))) pushes the whole panel outward by
           4 mm * sqrt(2) =~ 5.66 mm and opens a gap at the back-wall seam;
           the values below place the inner-face corner at exactly (0, 200),
           coincident with the back wall's edge. -->
      <visual name="left_wing_visual">
        <pose>0.06788 0.27354 0.150 0 0 0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.008 0.300</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.62 0.62 0.62 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.90 0.90 0.89 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.04 0.04 0.04 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="left_wing_collision">
        <pose>0.06788 0.27354 0.150 0 0 0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.008 0.300</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- Right wing: the left wing mirrored in Y. -->
      <visual name="right_wing_visual">
        <pose>0.06788 -0.27354 0.150 0 0 -0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.008 0.300</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.62 0.62 0.62 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.90 0.90 0.89 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.04 0.04 0.04 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision name="right_wing_collision">
        <pose>0.06788 -0.27354 0.150 0 0 -0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.008 0.300</size></box></geometry>
      </collision>

      <!-- ========================= accent band ========================= -->

      <!-- A dark band wrapping all three inner faces at z 200..210, 1 mm proud
           of the wall the way applied vinyl would be. This is the one styling
           element on the surface the robot actually looks at, and it is what
           keeps the wall from being a blank field.

           z 200..210 is chosen, not convenient: the bottom edge is 20 mm
           above the 180 mm ceiling of the mandated LiDAR band, and 35.5 mm
           above the tag plate's top edge at 164.5. The band's thickness is
           what's left over: the 100 mm free zone above z=200 has to hold the
           band, the letter at its full 72 mm height, and a 9 mm clearance on
           each side of the letter, which leaves 10 mm for the band. No
           collision - it is paint, not structure.

           One overlay per wing, sized to the wing's full 200 mm length. -->
      <visual name="back_wall_band_visual">
        <pose>0.0005 0 0.205 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.001 0.400 0.010</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.050 0.050 0.055 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.085 0.085 0.095 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.02 0.02 0.02 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <visual name="left_wing_band_visual">
        <pose>0.07106 0.27036 0.205 0 0 0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.001 0.010</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.050 0.050 0.055 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.085 0.085 0.095 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.02 0.02 0.02 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <visual name="right_wing_band_visual">
        <pose>0.07106 -0.27036 0.205 0 0 -0.785398</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.200 0.001 0.010</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.050 0.050 0.055 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.085 0.085 0.095 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.02 0.02 0.02 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- ============================ letter ============================ -->

      <!-- Human-readable dock identity, dock @DOCK_LETTER@. Sits on the back
           wall's own free zone above the accent band rather than on a
           separate pylon, so it costs no extra panel.

           96 x 72 mm, the same 4:3 aspect as the source letter_*.png (960 x
           720) so the glyph isn't stretched. Centred at z=255: that leaves a
           9 mm gap above the band's top edge (210) and a 9 mm gap below the
           wall's own top edge (300), so the plate clears both without
           touching either. 1 mm proud at x=0.0005, the same convention as
           the accent band and the tag, and likewise no collision - paint,
           not structure.

           This is dock @DOCK_LETTER@, and the letter always matches the tag
           id because CMakeLists.txt derives DOCK_LETTER_TEXTURE and
           DOCK_TAG_TEXTURE from the same DOCK_ASSIGNMENTS entry - the two
           markings cannot drift apart. -->
      <visual name="dock_letter_visual">
        <pose>0.0005 0 0.255 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.001 0.096 0.072</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>1 1 1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>1 1 1 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0 0 0 1</specular>
          <pbr>
            <metal>
              <albedo_map>model://@DOCK_MODEL@/materials/textures/@DOCK_LETTER_TEXTURE@</albedo_map>
              <metalness>0</metalness>
              <roughness>0.9</roughness>
            </metal>
          </pbr>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- ============================= tag ============================= -->

      <!-- AprilTag tag36h11 id 0, centred at z=130 mm - 28 mm above the
           OAK-D Lite's optical axis (oak_d_lite_link on base_footprint sits
           at z=101.953 mm). The deciding constraints are the 7.5 mm gap to
           the accent band above it (plate top 192.5 mm, band bottom 200 mm)
           and the 80-180 mm LiDAR band below. A tag mounted much higher
           would force a camera mast, which then punches a hole in the
           robot's own LiDAR scan plane; 130 mm stays clear of that.

           Minimum standoff for the whole plate to be visible is around
           64-100 mm depending on how far the tag sits off the camera's
           optical axis at a given range; horizontal never binds first, since
           the 34.5 deg half-FOV alone would allow standoffs down to 32 mm.
           Re-measure in sim if the exact figure matters for the 150-250 mm
           scoring window.

           The plate is 125 mm square, NOT 100 mm. The source PNG is 10 x 10
           cells - an 8 x 8 tag inside a 1-cell white margin - so the black
           border square covers 8/10 of the plate: 125 * 8/10 = 100 mm, which is
           the figure apriltag_ros wants in `size` (size: 0.100). Sizing the
           plate to 100 mm would give an 80 mm black square and put every
           downstream pose estimate 20 % out.

           The texture is upscaled from 10 x 10 to 1000 x 1000 with
           nearest-neighbour only; any smooth filter blurs the cell edges and
           the detector stops seeing it.

           1 mm thick and centred at x=0.0005, so the visible face sits 1 mm
           proud of the wall's inner face and cannot z-fight with it. A detector
           therefore reports the tag plane at x=1 mm, not x=0. No collision
           geometry - the tag is paint, not structure.

           The plate spans z 67.5..192.5 mm, fully covering the 80-180 mm
           LiDAR band. That is a print consideration, not a modelling one:
           gz-sim's gpu_lidar ignores material, and the plate is only 1 mm
           proud, well under the LD19P's 15 mm range noise. On the physical
           dock, matte black paper in the scan band returns more weakly than
           the surrounding light panel across the whole band.

           No collision with the letter decal above: plate top at z=192.5 mm,
           letter bottom at z=219 mm (see "letter" above) - 26.5 mm clear.

           This dock carries id @DOCK_TAG_ID@. -->
      <visual name="apriltag_visual">
        <pose>0.0005 0 0.130 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.001 0.125 0.125</size></box></geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>1 1 1 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>1 1 1 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0 0 0 1</specular>
          <pbr>
            <metal>
              <albedo_map>model://@DOCK_MODEL@/materials/textures/@DOCK_TAG_TEXTURE@</albedo_map>
              <metalness>0</metalness>
              <roughness>0.9</roughness>
            </metal>
          </pbr>
        </material>
      </visual>

    </link>

    <!-- Pose of a correctly docked robot, in the dock frame. Delivery is
         defined as arrival here; nothing is physically transferred.

         This is the pose of the rover's MODEL ORIGIN, which is base_footprint -
         the frame DiffDrive publishes odom against - not base_link.

         Derived from einride_mini_truck_description rather than assumed. The
         rover's front-most point is the front wheel outer edge: wheel link at
         x=+0.08596 with a mesh reaching +0.040, so +0.12596 ahead of
         base_footprint (the chassis mesh itself stops at +0.12306, and
         base_link sits 0.46 mm ahead of base_footprint). Overall length is
         0.2510 m and base_footprint is centred in it to within 0.5 mm.
         Front face 200 mm off the back wall therefore puts base_footprint at
         0.200 + 0.12596 = 0.32596, i.e. 0.326 to the nearest millimetre.

         Yaw 180 deg: the robot's +X points at the back wall.

         Scoring tolerance is standoff 150-250 mm, yaw error < 6 deg,
         stationary for 2 s - so the 200 mm nominal has 50 mm either way. -->
    <frame name="dock_approach_pose">
      <pose>0.326 0 0 0 0 3.14159265</pose>
    </frame>

  </model>
</sdf>
